, and lies below it outside. Their ratio is least, $14/39$, at $r = (39\\alpha m/10)^{1/3} = 1.696\\,m$, just inside $r_+$. Far out $K$ approaches Schwarzschild's.","At $r_b$, $R = -24/\\alpha = -19.2/m^2$. $R = -6\\alpha m^2/r^6$ is negative at every $r$, and Schwarzschild's vanishes. The area $\\alpha$ is of the order of the Planck length squared, so at $\\alpha = 5m^2/4$ the mass $M$ is of the order of the Planck mass. For a black hole of a star's mass, $K$ differs from Schwarzschild's by more than a percent only far inside $r_+$."],"ricci_sentences":[[1,0],[1,1]],"per_scalar":{"kretschmann":{"opening":"The Kretschmann scalar $K$ of the quantum Oppenheimer-Snyder black hole against $r$ ($\\alpha = 5m^2/4$), the same at every $t$, $\\theta$, and $\\phi$, beside Schwarzschild's black hole of the same mass.","clip":"We draw $K\\,m^4$ on a logarithmic scale from $10^{-4}$ to $10^{4}$ and cut each curve where it leaves its panel, as Schwarzschild's $K$ does inside $r = 0.411\\,m$.","legend":[["line","K","$K$, the Kretschmann scalar"],["line","reference","$K$ of Schwarzschild's black hole of the same mass"],["line","horizon","a horizon, where $g^{rr} = 0$"]]},"ricci_scalar":{"opening":"The Ricci scalar $R$ of the quantum Oppenheimer-Snyder black hole against $r$ ($\\alpha = 5m^2/4$), the same at every $t$, $\\theta$, and $\\phi$.","clip":"We draw $R\\,m^2$ on a signed logarithmic scale, linear within $10^{-5}$ of zero, from $-10^{2}$ to $0$; no curve leaves its panel.","legend":[["line","R","$R$, the Ricci scalar"],["line","horizon","a horizon, where $g^{rr} = 0$"]]}},"settings":"$m = 1$, the unit of every length, and $\\alpha = 5m^2/4$, so that $r_+ = 1.777\\,m$, $r_- = 1.127\\,m$, and $r_b = 0.855\\,m$.","clip":"We draw $K\\,m^4$ on a logarithmic scale from $10^{-4}$ to $10^{4}$ and $R\\,m^2$ on a signed logarithmic scale, linear within $10^{-5}$ of zero, from $-10^{2}$ to $0$, and cut each curve where it leaves its panel, as Schwarzschild's $K$ does inside $r = 0.411\\,m$."}]}
